p.src:
The Lorentz transformation is $t'=\gamma(t-vx),
\qquad
x'=\gamma(x-vt),
\qquad
\gamma=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-v^2}}.$ Choose the primed simultaneity slice $t'=0$. Equation  then gives $t=vx.$ The first crest intersects this slice at the origin. The second crest obeys $x=t+1$, so its intersection satisfies $\begin{aligned}
x&=vx+1,\\
x&=\frac{1}{1-v},
\qquad
t=\frac{v}{1-v}.\end{aligned}$ Its primed coordinate is $\begin{aligned}
x'
&=\frac{x-vt}{\sqrt{1-v^2}}\\
&=\frac{1-v^2}{(1-v)\sqrt{1-v^2}}\\
&=\sqrt{\frac{1+v}{1-v}}.\end{aligned}$ Since the original wavelength was one, $\boxed{
\frac{\lambda'}{\lambda}
=
\sqrt{\frac{1+\beta}{1-\beta}}
},
\qquad
\beta=\frac{v}{c}.$ Equivalently, $\frac{\lambda'}{\lambda}
=
\sqrt{\frac{c+v}{c-v}}.$

